{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>When Elyon gave the nations as an inheritance.</b> Meaning: when HaShem blessed be He gave the nations as an inheritance to the celestial princes — this declares that they are inferior before Him, for He did not want them to be called by His name, and He gave them to the celestial princes." "<b>When He separated the children of man.</b> Meaning: HaShem did not deliver all the nations into the hand of one prince, but rather separated them and gave a portion to each and every prince. And the manner of division He made among them when He established their boundary was according to the number of the children of Israel. That is, you should know that every act of HaShem — He made this opposite that, good and evil, and all the souls among the created beings, HaShem planted from two trees: one tree good and one evil. The good tree is Adam haRishon; the evil tree is Samael, a man of worthlessness (בליעל). And when Adam haRishon sinned and evil became mixed with good, there once again emerged from the offspring of Adam haRishon also evil souls, until there emerged one pure soul in which were included all the holy neshamot, and the good tree was renewedly clarified — and this is Jacob our father, peace be upon him. Therefore they said (Bava Metzi'a 84a; Zohar vol. 3 244a): שׁוּפְרֵיהּ דְּיַעֲקֹב אָבִינוּ מֵעֵין שׁוּפְרֵיהּ דְּאָדָם קַדְמָאָה — \"the beauty of Jacob our father was like the beauty of the first man,\" and understand the matter. And this tree has seventy branches, and they are the seventy souls that went down to Egypt. Therefore, when HaShem separated the nations, He established their boundary according to the number of the children of Israel — He made them seventy, corresponding to the seventy branches of holiness." "<b>And the intention</b> in this is that every aspect and aspect of each branch of holiness should rule over its counterpart in the realm of evil. And this is the sod of (Proverbs 5:5): ״רַגְלֶיהָ יוֹרְדוֹת מָוֶת״ — that the feet of holiness tread upon the heads of the kelipah, and the branches of Jacob are thus found to rule and reign over the nations. And He said ״כִּי חֵלֶק ה׳ עַמּוֹ״ — this explains the reason: since the nation that HaShem chose for Himself, that He Himself should be the one ruling over it, is Israel, therefore HaShem intended this and crowned and exalted the children of Israel above all the nations; and in this way, when He reigns over Israel, He reigns over the entire world. And His saying ״חֵלֶק ה׳ עַמּוֹ״ alludes to Israel after they received the Torah, for then they are called by the name עַם ה׳ (the people of HaShem). And His saying ״יַעֲקֹב חֶבֶל״ means the חֶבֶל (cord/portion) that is the head of the inheritance, and this is what I alerted you to earlier in the hint of the Sages, their memory for blessing (Bava Metzi'a 84a): שׁוּפְרֵיהּ דְּיַעֲקֹב — \"the beauty of Jacob,\" etc." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to say that there are two aspects in the souls: there is one that comes from the supernal world, and therefore it says ״חֵלֶק ה׳״ — meaning, a portion of HaShem blessed be He, as it were. And there are souls that come from the place called נַחֲלַת שַׁדַּי (the inheritance of Shaddai), and therefore Jacob is called ״חֶבֶל נַחֲלָתוֹ״. And He was precise in saying ״חֶבֶל״ to allude to a great matter that those who know grace (חֵן) have revealed: every vital soul (נֶפֶשׁ הַחִיּוּנִי) in man has one thread (חוּט) bound to its root, and it goes out from the nostrils and is bound to its root above, and from there the soul draws sustenance to live. And concerning the soul of the wicked, Scripture says (Numbers 15:31): ״הִכָּרֵת תִּכָּרֵת״ etc. — meaning, that root which is bound to the soul from the place from which it was hewn, HaShem will cut it off, and it no longer has sustenance — and this is death, may the Merciful One spare us. And concerning the righteous it is said (Deuteronomy 4:4): ״וְאַתֶּם הַדְּבֵקִים בַּה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם חַיִּים״ etc. — and this is His saying ״חֶבֶל נַחֲלָתוֹ״; and understand."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {2 "The Or HaChaim cites the Aramaic phrase שׁוּפְרֵיהּ דְּיַעֲקֹב ('the beauty of Jacob') from Bava Metzi'a 84a, here transliterated since the wordplay and reference depend on the Aramaic."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:21:27.916096508Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}